# Coding Practices

General standards for all code in this backend.

## TypeScript

- Write all code in TypeScript — no plain JavaScript files.
- Prefer explicit types on public APIs (service methods, DTOs, controller return types).
- Avoid `any`; use `unknown` and narrow when the type is uncertain.

## NestJS

- Follow NestJS module / controller / service patterns.
- Keep controllers thin; business logic belongs in services.
- Use dependency injection — never instantiate services or repositories manually in feature code.
- Load env with `ConfigModule.forRoot({ isGlobal: true })` in `AppModule` — not in feature modules.
- Enable CORS in `main.ts` from `CORS_ORIGIN` (comma-separated frontend origins). The SPA is a different origin; do not assume same-origin or a reverse proxy.
- Do not add a global `/api` prefix. This process *is* the API; routes stay at `/auth`, `/users`, etc.
- Do not serve the React build from NestJS. Do not add Docker or nginx for this app.
- See `api-conventions.mdc` for REST endpoint rules.
- See `saas-api.mdc` for public vs private resources, null handling, and ownership on every route.

## Prisma

- Use Prisma for all database access — see `db-interactions.mdc`.
- Inject `PrismaService`; do not create Prisma clients in feature modules.

## Production-Ready Code

- Ship code that is safe to run in production — no temporary debug logs, commented-out blocks, or dev-only shortcuts left behind.
- Do not leave `console.log` for debugging; use NestJS `Logger` when logging is intentional.
- Handle errors explicitly; do not swallow exceptions silently.
